# LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working directory, on every platform. This matches the Odin
# project's own .editorconfig, which declares `end_of_line = lf` despite Odin being developed mainly on
# Windows.
#
# `text=auto` alone - which is all this file used to say - only guarantees LF in the *index*; what lands
# in the working directory is then left to each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which Git for Windows sets
# to `true` in its system config. That gives Windows checkouts CRLF files while .editorconfig
# (end_of_line = lf) and the tools that rewrite files in place - odinfmt above all - emit LF, so a
# formatting pass leaves a file with mixed endings and Git reports "<line-endings changed since
# checkout>". Adding `eol=lf` pins the working directory too and overrides core.autocrlf, so the whole
# toolchain agrees. Odin, just, cmd and Sublime all read LF happily on Windows.
#
# Keep this in sync with .editorconfig's `end_of_line` and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the exception: some interpreters need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch the contents of these - the `* text=auto eol=lf` above would otherwise corrupt one that
# is committed deliberately or force-added, and this project ships a prebuilt rure.lib / librure.a.
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.lib binary
*.dll binary
*.obj binary
*.o binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.bc binary
*.spall binary
